  "verdict": "Agentic extraction is a good place to start for long lists. On synthetic statements with Claude Haiku 5.5, single-pass returned every row at 100 rows, but from 400 rows some statements came back with only the first page's rows (64% of rows at 400 rows, 2% at 1,600), and Amazon Bedrock rejected the 3,200-row requests as too long. Agentic extraction returned every row at every size with table parsing on, and 98% to 100% with it off. On these clean tables, table parsing also made agentic extraction cheaper: from 800 rows it cost less than single-pass even if every call billed at the long-context rate. Real invoices agree on agentic extraction, which found 67% of the line items against 17% for single-pass ([When is agentic extraction worth it?](#when-is-agentic-extraction-worth-it)), but not on table parsing, which didn't help on long real invoices ([Does deterministic table parsing help agentic extraction?](#does-deterministic-table-parsing-help-agentic-extraction)). Test it on your own documents.",
